  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🏡 Real Estate Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for an agency, brokerage or team, from Next Move Realty to Hartley & Rowe."
    },
    "intro": "Good real estate names sound trustworthy and local: a surname firm like <em>Bramley &amp; Co.</em>, a friendly idea like <em>Welcome Mat Property Co.</em>, or a team name like <em>The Walsh Home Team</em>. Name your agency, brokerage or team with this **Real Estate Name Generator**. It works for US realty firms and UK estate and letting agents alike: choose a style and your wording, or add your first name or town.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good real estate business names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Bramley &amp; Co.</strong>: short, solid and believable on a high street board.</li><li><strong>Welcome Mat Property Co.</strong>: a warm image for first-time buyers.</li><li><strong>Bricks &amp; Keys</strong>: two things every sale ends with.</li><li><strong>Blue Door Realty</strong>: a simple picture that's easy to remember from a sign.</li><li><strong>Beaumont Fine Homes</strong>: a luxury name for high-end listings.</li><li><strong>The Walsh Home Team</strong>: the usual US shape for an agent's team.</li><li><strong>Copper Beech Real Estate Team</strong>: a tree name that sounds settled and local.</li><li><strong>Properties by Sarah</strong>: an agent's own name, for a one-person business.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Estate agent or realtor? The words matter",
        "content": "<p>The same job has different names on each side of the Atlantic, and the right word helps people searching for you.</p><ul><li><strong>In the UK</strong> you're an <em>estate agent</em> if you sell and a <em>letting agent</em> if you rent. Names usually end in <em>Estate Agents</em>, <em>Property</em>, <em>Homes</em> or <em>Lettings</em>.</li><li><strong>In the US</strong> firms use <em>Realty</em>, <em>Real Estate</em>, <em>Properties</em> or <em>Group</em>. <em>REALTOR&reg;</em> is a registered trade mark of the National Association of Realtors, for its members, so don't use it in a business name unless you belong.</li><li><strong>Teams</strong> inside a brokerage often take the lead agent's surname: <em>The Ingram Team</em>. US states and brokerages have their own rules on team names, so check with your broker first.</li></ul><p>Choose <strong>US</strong> or <strong>UK</strong> in the generator and the names use the matching words. <strong>Works anywhere</strong> sticks to <em>Homes</em> and <em>Properties</em>, which read naturally in both.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>Every name here has a <strong>Check</strong> button, which opens the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>: it looks up domains, social handles, the company register and trade mark searches in one place. Before you order signs or cards:</p><ul><li><strong>Company register.</strong> In the UK, search Companies House; a limited company's name can't be the same as one already registered. In the US, search your state's business register. UK estate and letting agents must also belong to a government-approved redress scheme before they trade.</li><li><strong>Trade marks.</strong> Search the UK Intellectual Property Office or the USPTO for marks in your line of work. Registering a company doesn't give you trade mark rights.</li><li><strong>Domain and handles.</strong> Try the .co.uk or .com and the Instagram and Facebook names at the same time, and change the name slightly rather than settle for a messy handle.</li><li><strong>Local search.</strong> Look up the name with your town. Two similar businesses a few miles apart confuse customers and reviews.</li></ul><p>This is general guidance, not legal advice. For the full process, read <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>.</p><p>For other professional names, try the <a href=\"consulting-business-name-generator.html\">Consulting</a> and <a href=\"company-name-generator.html\">Company Name</a> generators, or the <a href=\"business-name-generator.html\">Business Name Generator</a> for a name in your own words.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_services-names.html\">professional and creative business name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are good real estate business names?",
        "answer": "Names that sound trustworthy and local: a surname firm like Bramley & Co., a warm idea like Welcome Mat Property Co., or a team name like The Walsh Home Team. Choose US or UK wording to match what clients search for."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I call my business \"Realtors\"?",
        "answer": "Only with care. REALTOR® is a trade mark of the US National Association of Realtors, for its members, so these names use \"Realty\" and \"Real Estate\" instead. US states and brokerages also have rules for team names (some ban words like \"Realty\" or \"Group\" for teams), so check with your broker and state licensing board. In the UK, an estate agency can use any name that isn't already taken."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's a good name for a real estate team?",
        "answer": "The lead agent's surname plus Team or Home Team (The Ingram Team) is the usual shape, because clients often follow an agent rather than a brand. Choose the Team or group style for more."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I check a real estate business name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. In the UK, search Companies House; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Check your local area too, as two similar names nearby confuse customers."
      }
    ]
  },
